WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
One protein marks a stripe of cells.

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.620
Does it command their fate?

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.080
Watching where it appears in an embryo cannot separate cause from label.

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.360
To test its power, put that protein into cells that normally lack it.

5
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:23.723
Making a protein in cells that normally lack it is called misexpression, or ectopic

6
00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:29.723
expression. To test a gene in a chick neural tube, researchers inject its DNA

7
00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:35.383
inside. At first, that DNA floats in the hollow center, outside the cells.

8
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:41.867
Next, brief electric pulses push DNA into cells on one side of the tube.

9
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:44.387
That is electroporation.

10
00:00:45.787 --> 00:00:50.267
Only the pulsed side makes the protein; the opposite side stays an untouched

11
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:56.747
control. Adding a factor elsewhere tests sufficiency: what it can impose, not what

12
00:00:56.747 --> 00:00:57.747
tissue needs.

13
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.010
Predict a result here.

14
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.390
Just above the floor plate, stripe p3 makes Nkx2.2.

15
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:10.150
The stripe above, pMN, carries a low level of Pax6.

16
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.850
Each factor switches the other off at their border.

17
00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:17.330
Now pulse it into pMN on the right.

18
00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.410
What happens to Pax6 and the border?

19
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:30.633
On the pulsed right side, Nkx2.2 switches Pax6 off.

20
00:01:31.813 --> 00:01:34.373
The border shifts upward into what was pMN.

21
00:01:35.673 --> 00:01:39.993
Meanwhile, the untouched left side holds its normal border in the same embryo.

22
00:01:41.573 --> 00:01:46.573
Briscoe and colleagues used chick electroporation like this to map opposing borders.

23
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.833
Still, misexpression can mislead.

24
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:56.113
A factor is sufficient only in the cells and stage tested, using their existing

25
00:01:56.113 --> 00:02:02.113
machinery. Shifting a border in one tissue cannot prove a gene builds an organizer

26
00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:06.813
anywhere. Nor does it prove the embryo normally needs that gene.

27
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:10.347
Paired controls guard against both traps.

28
00:02:11.587 --> 00:02:16.667
To test necessity, researchers pair gain with loss: they delete the gene or block

29
00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:22.467
its receptor. To verify the pathway, they repeat delivery in tissue lacking the

30
00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:27.567
receptor. If nothing happens there, the effect runs through that pathway.

31
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:33.150
With those controls, misexpression unlocked how embryos pattern brains.

32
00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:40.370
In 1993, Yann Echelard, Andrew McMahon and colleagues forced Sonic hedgehog on in

33
00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:45.650
mice. Where the central nervous system lacked it, floor-plate genes switched on.

34
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:53.370
In 2001, Sato, Araki and Nakamura misexpressed FGF8b in chick embryos.

35
00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.750
That signal could turn midbrain roof into cerebellum.

36
00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:05.333
In 2002, Anjen Chenn and Christopher Walsh expressed stabilized beta β-catenin in

37
00:03:05.333 --> 00:03:06.633
mouse neural progenitors.

38
00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:10.593
Stabilized means the cell does not break it down.

39
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:16.733
More of those cells kept dividing, enlarging and folding the normally smooth cortex.

40
00:03:17.973 --> 00:03:23.093
That showed extra divisions can enlarge the cortex, not how primate cortex grows.

41
00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:28.813
Adding shows what a factor can command; deleting shows what tissue requires.
